%% Fact Book section for Topic 2 — included by content/FB/main.gsm under a "Topic 2" H1.

## T2 · Dates and milestones

::: table | Topic 2 timeline.
@widths 1.6,12.4
@font 9.5
Year | Event
1762 | Rousseau, *Émile* — girls to be educated to please men
1790 | Condorcet, *On the Admission of Women to the Rights of Citizenship*
1791 | Olympe de Gouges, *Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen* (executed 1793)
1792 | Mary Wollstonecraft, *A Vindication of the Rights of Woman*
1829 | *Sati* banned in Bengal after Raja Ram Mohan Roy's campaign
1848 | Seneca Falls Convention; Declaration of Sentiments (Stanton, Mott; signed by Frederick Douglass)
1851 | Sojourner Truth speaks at Akron, Ohio ("Ain't I a Woman?" version published 1863); Harriet Taylor Mill, *The Enfranchisement of Women*
1856 | Hindu Widows' Remarriage Act (Vidyasagar's campaign)
1867 | J. S. Mill proposes "person" for "man" in the Reform Bill
1869 | J. S. Mill, *The Subjection of Women*
1877 | Lewis Henry Morgan, *Ancient Society*
1879 | August Bebel, *Woman and Socialism*
1882 | Hubertine Auclert calls herself a "feminist" (*La Citoyenne*); Tarabai Shinde, *Stri Purush Tulana*
1884 | Engels, *The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State*
1893 | New Zealand: women win the national vote
1898 | Sayyid Mumtaz Ali, *Huquq-un-Niswan* (Lahore)
1899 | Qasim Amin, *The Liberation of Women* (Egypt)
1905 | Begum Rokeya Sakhawat Hossain, *Sultana's Dream*
1906 | Sheikh Abdullah ("Papa Mian") founds a girls' school at Aligarh (later Aligarh Women's College)
1910 | Clara Zetkin proposes International Women's Day (Copenhagen); first held 1911
1911 | Begum Rokeya founds a girls' school in Calcutta
1913 | Rebecca West's "doormat" remark on feminism
1918 / 1928 | Britain: partial, then equal, votes for women
1920 | USA: 19th Amendment (women's vote)
1923 | Huda Shaarawi founds the Egyptian Feminist Union
1924 / 1925 | Freud: "Anatomy is destiny" (1924); penis envy and the weaker female superego (1925)
1926 | Karen Horney, "The Flight from Womanhood" — womb envy
1944 | Jinnah at Aligarh (10 March): "No nation can rise to the height of glory unless your women are side by side with you"
1949 | Simone de Beauvoir, *The Second Sex*; APWA founded by Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan
1963 | Betty Friedan, *The Feminine Mystique*; US Equal Pay Act
1964 | US Civil Rights Act, Title VII (no sex discrimination in employment)
1966 | NOW founded (Friedan first president); Juliet Mitchell, "Women: The Longest Revolution"
1967 | New York Radical Women; Derrida, *Of Grammatology*
1968 | Miss America protest ("freedom trash can"); Martha Weinman Lear, "The Second Feminist Wave"
1969 | Redstockings Manifesto; Margaret Benston, "The Political Economy of Women's Liberation"; Hanisch writes "The Personal Is Political"
1970 | Kate Millett, *Sexual Politics*; Shulamith Firestone, *The Dialectic of Sex*; Robin Morgan (ed.), *Sisterhood Is Powerful*; Hanisch essay published
1971 | Juliet Mitchell, *Woman's Estate*; first modern women's shelter (Chiswick, London)
1972 | Dalla Costa and James, *The Power of Women and the Subversion of the Community*; Wages for Housework launched
1973 | Chipko movement begins (Uttarakhand, India)
1974 | Françoise d'Eaubonne coins "ecofeminism"; Juliet Mitchell, *Psychoanalysis and Feminism*; Irigaray, *Speculum*; Kristeva, *Revolution in Poetic Language*
1975 | Cixous, "The Laugh of the Medusa" (écriture féminine); Brownmiller, *Against Our Will*; Federici, "Wages Against Housework"; Mernissi, *Beyond the Veil*; UN begins marking IWD (8 March); Foucault, *Discipline and Punish*
1976 | Dinnerstein, *The Mermaid and the Minotaur*; Rich, *Of Woman Born*; *DeGraffenreid v. General Motors*
1977 | Combahee River Collective Statement; Irigaray, *This Sex Which Is Not One*; Showalter, *A Literature of Their Own*; Green Belt Movement (Maathai)
1978 | Chodorow, *The Reproduction of Mothering*; Mary Daly, *Gyn/Ecology*
1979 | Hartmann, "The Unhappy Marriage of Marxism and Feminism"; Eisenstein (ed.), *Capitalist Patriarchy*; MacKinnon, *Sexual Harassment of Working Women*; Showalter coins "gynocritics"; Lyotard, *The Postmodern Condition*; Lorde, "The Master's Tools"; CEDAW adopted; Hudood Ordinances (Pakistan)
1980 | Rich, "Compulsory Heterosexuality"; Merchant, *The Death of Nature*; Iris Marion Young, "Beyond the Unhappy Marriage"
1981 | bell hooks, *Ain't I a Woman*; Eisenstein, *The Radical Future of Liberal Feminism*; Women's Action Forum founded
1982 | Gilligan, *In a Different Voice*; Sindhiani Tehreek begins
1983 | Hartsock, "The Feminist Standpoint"; Alice Walker coins "womanism"; Lise Vogel on social reproduction; Dworkin–MacKinnon anti-pornography ordinance
1984 | hooks, *Feminist Theory: From Margin to Center*; Mohanty, "Under Western Eyes"
1985 | Haraway, "A Cyborg Manifesto"
1986 | Bhasin and Khan, *Some Questions on Feminism and its Relevance in South Asia*; Mies, *Patriarchy and Accumulation*; Harding, *The Science Question in Feminism*; Scott, "Gender: A Useful Category"
1987 | Flax, "Postmodernism and Gender Relations"; Weedon, *Feminist Practice and Poststructuralist Theory*; Dorothy Smith, *The Everyday World as Problematic*; Mernissi, *The Veil and the Male Elite* (French edition)
1988 | Kandiyoti, "Bargaining with Patriarchy"; Spivak, "Can the Subaltern Speak?"; Shiva, *Staying Alive*; Scott, "Deconstructing Equality-versus-Difference"; Fraser and Nicholson; Sisters in Islam (Malaysia)
1989 | Crenshaw coins intersectionality; Okin, *Justice, Gender, and the Family*; Tong, *Feminist Thought*; Hochschild, *The Second Shift*; Montreal massacre (6 December)
1990 | Butler, *Gender Trouble*; Collins, *Black Feminist Thought*; Walby, *Theorizing Patriarchy*; Bly, *Iron John*
1991 | Crenshaw, "Mapping the Margins"; White Ribbon Campaign (Canada)
1992 | Wadud, *Qur'an and Woman*; Leila Ahmed, *Women and Gender in Islam*; Schüssler Fiorenza coins kyriarchy (*But She Said*); Agarwal, "The Gender and Environment Debate"; *Zanan* (Iran); Rebecca Walker's "Third Wave"
1993 | Mies and Shiva, *Ecofeminism*; Butler, *Bodies That Matter*
1994 | ICPD Cairo (male responsibility); Lady Health Worker Programme (Pakistan)
1995 | Connell, *Masculinities*; *Feminist Contentions* (Benhabib, Butler and others); Beijing Platform for Action
1996 | Pakistan accedes to CEDAW; Kimmel, *Manhood in America*
1997 | Messner, *Politics of Masculinities*; Narayan, *Dislocating Cultures*
1999 | Nussbaum, "The Professor of Parody"; Moghissi, *Feminism and Islamic Fundamentalism*
2000 | hooks, *Feminism Is for Everybody*; Nussbaum, *Women and Human Development*; NCSW (Pakistan); Okara tenants' movement begins
2002 | Barlas, *"Believing Women" in Islam*; Abu-Lughod, "Do Muslim Women Really Need Saving?"
2004 | hooks, *The Will to Change*; MenEngage Alliance; Maathai's Nobel Peace Prize
2005 | Saba Mahmood, *Politics of Piety*
2006 | Tarana Burke first uses "Me Too"
2008 | Benazir Income Support Programme
2009 | Musawah launched (Kuala Lumpur)
2012 | Supreme Court orders regularisation of Lady Health Workers
2014 | HeForShe launched (20 September); Malala Yousafzai wins the Nobel Peace Prize
2017 | #MeToo spreads worldwide; Bhattacharya (ed.), *Social Reproduction Theory*
2018 | First Aurat March (8 March); Women Democratic Front founded; Sindh Home-Based Workers Act; Afiya Zia, *Faith and Feminism in Pakistan*; Ziauddin Yousafzai, *Let Her Fly*
2019–20 | Controversy over the slogan *Mera Jism Meri Marzi*
2022 | Floods affect about 33 million people in Pakistan
2025 | Islamabad Child Marriage Restraint Act (minimum age 18)
:::

## T2 · The schools in one table

::: table | Each school's cause, remedy and founding texts.
@widths 2.4,3.4,3.2,5
@font 9.5
School | Cause | Remedy | Founding texts
Liberal | Unequal laws and chances | Reform; equal rights | Wollstonecraft 1792; Mill 1869; Friedan 1963
Radical | Patriarchy | Transform at the root | Millett 1970; Firestone 1970; Hanisch 1970
Marxist | Capitalism, private property | End capitalism; socialise care | Engels 1884; Bebel 1879
Socialist | Capitalism + patriarchy | Change economy and family | Mitchell 1971; Hartmann 1979; Eisenstein 1979
Psychoanalytic | Unconscious; early childhood | Shared parenting | Horney 1926; Mitchell 1974; Chodorow 1978
Men's | Patriarchy harms men too | Men as allies | Connell 1995; Kimmel 1996; hooks 2004
Postmodern | Fixed categories; discourse | Deconstruct; value difference | Cixous 1975; Flax 1987; Butler 1990
Intersectional / Black | Interlocking oppressions | Target the overlaps | Combahee 1977; Crenshaw 1989; Collins 1990
Postcolonial | Western and colonial dominance | Local feminisms | Mohanty 1984; Spivak 1988
Ecofeminist | Domination of women and nature | Care-based, sustainable economy | d'Eaubonne 1974; Shiva 1988
Muslim | Patriarchal interpretation | Re-read the sources | Mernissi 1987; Wadud 1992; Barlas 2002
:::

## T2 · Coined terms and their authors

::: table | Who coined or developed what (the MCQ favourites).
@widths 4,4,6
@font 9.5
Term | Author (year) | Meaning
Feminist (as self-description) | Hubertine Auclert (1882) | Advocate of women's rights
Sexual politics | Kate Millett (1970) | Power relations between the sexes
Sex class | Shulamith Firestone (1970) | Women as a class defined by sex
The personal is political | Carol Hanisch (1970) | Private life shaped by power
Womb envy | Karen Horney (1926) | Men's envy of women's power to give birth
Dual-systems theory | Heidi Hartmann (1979) | Capitalism and patriarchy as two linked systems
Capitalist patriarchy | Zillah Eisenstein (1979) | The combined system
Housewifisation | Maria Mies (1986) | Women's work everywhere made secondary and cheap
Second shift | Arlie Hochschild (1989) | Housework after paid work
Écriture féminine | Hélène Cixous (1975) | Women's writing from the body
Gynocriticism | Elaine Showalter (1979) | Study of women writers
Ecofeminism | Françoise d'Eaubonne (1974) | Women's and nature's domination linked
Feminist environmentalism | Bina Agarwal (1992) | Women's link to nature through material work
Intersectionality | Kimberlé Crenshaw (1989) | Overlapping identities, unique disadvantage
Matrix of domination | Patricia Hill Collins (1990) | Organisation of intersecting oppressions
Womanism | Alice Walker (1983) | Black women's feminism
Strong objectivity | Sandra Harding (1986–91) | Objectivity improved by marginalised views
Strategic essentialism | Gayatri Spivak (1980s) | Temporary unity around an identity
Patriarchal bargain | Deniz Kandiyoti (1988) | Women's strategies within patriarchy
Kyriarchy | Elisabeth Schüssler Fiorenza (1992) | Interlocking structures of domination
Patriarchal dividend | R. W. Connell (1995) | Men's gain from women's subordination
Incredulity toward metanarratives | Jean-François Lyotard (1979) | Definition of the postmodern
:::

